Also, to add to what you were saying about the Commando controls, if you're able to turn and face other directions, that A-B mechanic is going to get real confusing real fast if you're facing other directions.

00:58:10.860 --> 00:58:14.860
And like, which direction does A shoot now when I'm facing this way and which direction does A shoot when I'm facing that way?

00:58:15.580 --> 00:58:18.060
So that would be an issue.

00:58:19.000 --> 00:58:29.640
Cool, cool. And I think kind of where I stand on that is after hearing this whole thing, I kind of wonder what Commando would feel like if it was on rails.

00:58:29.860 --> 00:58:31.920
I think I would like Commando a lot better.

00:58:32.120 --> 00:58:32.940
If it was on the rails.

00:58:32.940 --> 00:58:40.820
I think there's just something too about, especially in like these early games, the way that it presents itself when it's on rails.

00:58:41.800 --> 00:58:44.200
You know, like how enemies enter and approach.

00:58:44.400 --> 00:58:50.400
And like, I just think one of the problems with Commando was is that the way things spawn in and stuff.

00:58:50.620 --> 00:58:52.280
That game was a graphical mess too.

00:58:52.420 --> 00:58:54.380
Right, right. It was just that. There are a lot of other flaws with Commando.

00:58:54.380 --> 00:58:59.480
No, no, no, but I'm saying like it happens because you're more in control of things.

00:58:59.600 --> 00:59:05.060
Like I wonder that, you know, if it would have put itself on rails, if it would have had a better opportunity to be like,

00:59:05.200 --> 00:59:10.760
these people are going to be specifically behind bunkers and these guys are going to come out from the water at this point, you know?

00:59:10.840 --> 00:59:18.760
But because you were in free control of how you moved, you didn't really, you know, like the game couldn't time those things out for you.

00:59:18.920 --> 00:59:21.980
This game was made by Yoshiki Akimoto.

00:59:23.080 --> 00:59:24.380
Does that ring a bell?

00:59:24.660 --> 00:59:32.060
We talked about Yoshiki on the 1942 episode like a year and a half ago.

00:59:32.580 --> 00:59:39.160
Oh, man. The only reason why that's important is because Akimoto is the creator of Street Fighter 2.

00:59:39.760 --> 00:59:45.700
Oh, yeah. So the creator of Street Fighter 2 has not made that game yet, but has made Gunsmoke at this time.

00:59:46.800 --> 00:59:48.580
The prequel to Street Fighter 2.

00:59:48.880 --> 00:59:50.380
Yeah, yeah, absolutely.

00:59:50.380 --> 00:59:52.300
Absolutely. He's improving.

00:59:52.580 --> 00:59:53.780
Yeah, I think so.

00:59:54.400 --> 01:00:00.480
I think a lot of the things that, you know, like 1942's best thing for it was the aesthetic of it.

01:00:00.560 --> 01:00:02.000
I always liked that.

01:00:02.420 --> 01:00:03.780
And the theme song, obviously.

01:00:04.060 --> 01:00:06.840
Yeah, obviously. Yeah, that catchy music.

01:00:07.840 --> 01:00:09.680
Actually, this game has okay.

01:00:09.820 --> 01:00:10.840
It says good music, yeah.

01:00:10.840 --> 01:00:21.000
Yeah. But I think, like, that was maybe the only lesson that transferred over here because there's very little of 1942 that shows up in this game.

01:00:21.060 --> 01:00:26.800
And maybe that's because it came later in life and they were able to learn from other shmups, too.

01:00:27.160 --> 01:00:28.100
Also, you're not a plane.

01:00:28.740 --> 01:00:30.180
Yeah, yeah. That helps as well.

01:00:30.400 --> 01:00:32.600
That's big. Yeah. There's no, like, power roll.

01:00:32.600 --> 01:00:33.580
You can't do a back flip.

01:00:33.740 --> 01:00:35.020
Yeah. There's none of that.

01:00:36.160 --> 01:00:39.460
So, Akimoto's learning, but not even making fighting games.

01:00:39.460 --> 01:00:42.900
So, we'll see how Gunsmoke comes into play in Street Fighter.

01:00:43.440 --> 01:00:46.800
Are we? I don't think so.

01:00:47.740 --> 01:00:54.460
And then, before we get to the essential games list, I do have a, like, a cliff note for the sequels and spinoffs.

01:00:54.620 --> 01:00:57.500
This game did not get any officially.

01:00:57.860 --> 01:01:01.360
Mm-hmm. The Amstrad CPC.

01:01:02.480 --> 01:01:03.520
Amstrad CPC.

01:01:03.840 --> 01:01:05.540
Yes. That's a computer.

01:01:06.500 --> 01:01:09.100
Like a fax machine or something?

01:01:09.100 --> 01:01:16.900
Yeah, I mean, like, imagine, like, an 8-bit computer back in those days, but made by Amstrad.

01:01:17.040 --> 01:01:19.040
So, I think that was, like, European-focused.

01:01:19.040 --> 01:01:20.240
Okay. That market.

01:01:20.380 --> 01:01:21.780
So, we didn't really get those in the States.

01:01:22.920 --> 01:01:29.220
The game got many ports, and one of them was on the Amstrad CPC, and it got renamed to Desperado.

01:01:29.840 --> 01:01:34.220
But it's pretty much the same game with just absolutely terrible graphics.

01:01:36.340 --> 01:01:43.920
But it did so well, I guess, on the Amstrad that they made a sequel for Desperado called Desperado 2,

01:01:43.920 --> 01:01:51.980
which I guess you could consider a Gunsmoke sequel as well if you just, like, want to hold, you know, like, the ties as loose as possible.

01:01:52.280 --> 01:01:53.300
You just want to be like, well.

01:01:53.540 --> 01:01:54.640
Was it just the same game?

01:01:54.940 --> 01:02:00.360
Yeah, yeah. Desperado 2 is even worse, if you can imagine it, on the graphic fidelity side.

01:02:00.360 --> 01:02:04.980
But it also turned it into, like, a side-scrolling platformer.

01:02:05.680 --> 01:02:09.820
Okay. So, you know, it just doesn't.

01:02:09.820 --> 01:02:15.840
To me, it's just a Western game that has a Desperado name instead of Gunsmoke.

01:02:15.960 --> 01:02:18.700
So, I didn't even think of them as sequels and spinoffs, but I thought it was worth checking out.

01:02:18.800 --> 01:02:23.080
I'll put a link to both games in the show notes so you guys can check out the videos.

01:02:23.800 --> 01:02:24.900
Any stray thoughts?

01:02:25.420 --> 01:02:27.300
I have some stray thoughts.

01:02:28.620 --> 01:02:32.300
And all of them kind of pertain to the polish of this game.

01:02:32.400 --> 01:02:38.940
Because I don't think we have conveyed, at least the way I feel, is exactly how polished this game is.

01:02:39.320 --> 01:02:41.340
Like, I feel like it is the music.

01:02:41.340 --> 01:02:42.600
You guys mentioned the music's pretty good.

01:02:42.640 --> 01:02:43.980
I thought the music was really good.

01:02:44.240 --> 01:02:50.260
I thought the music, not only that it was, like, just good music tracks, but it was immersive.

01:02:50.480 --> 01:02:51.760
It felt like Western music.

01:02:51.760 --> 01:02:52.960
It did sound like Western music, yeah.

01:02:54.300 --> 01:03:02.920
But the art of everything felt very clean and something that's kind of rare on a lot of these NES games, especially the shmups.

01:03:03.500 --> 01:03:11.480
And especially at the very beginning of the game, you read a little scrolling bio or intro to the story.

01:03:11.920 --> 01:03:14.900
And as you're doing it, I don't know why I found this so charming.

01:03:15.240 --> 01:03:20.240
As you're reading it, there's a guy on a horse in the distance slowly getting closer and closer.

01:03:20.460 --> 01:03:25.040
And, like, I'm realizing, as I'm reading it, I'm realizing in my peripheral vision that he's getting closer and closer.

01:03:25.180 --> 01:03:26.540
And I'm like, oh, this is so fucking cool.

01:03:28.240 --> 01:03:33.020
So, as I'm going through, I'm just, I was pretty charmed by this game.

01:03:33.300 --> 01:03:38.520
And it takes a lot for me to be pretty high on a shmup.

01:03:38.780 --> 01:03:41.280
Yeah. Because we all know how much I don't like shmups.

01:03:41.660 --> 01:03:47.260
Okay. So, I think that this game had a lot of polish, like, that a lot of shmups don't.

01:03:48.220 --> 01:03:51.940
Very cool. I think with that in mind, Sean?

01:03:53.120 --> 01:03:54.600
I will sing.

01:03:56.100 --> 01:04:02.920
Instead of subjecting everybody to Sean's singing, we will go into the Essential Games List.

01:04:05.480 --> 01:04:12.840
This Essential Games List comes with a very interesting turn of events as we are going to include some listener mail.

01:04:13.160 --> 01:04:14.020
Listener mail.

01:04:14.160 --> 01:04:15.740
Into the Essential Games List this time.

01:04:15.740 --> 01:04:18.820
It comes from Sam Smith from New York.

01:04:19.640 --> 01:04:24.840
Sam could not be on the episode, but he did write in with his thoughts on the Essential Games List.

01:04:25.060 --> 01:04:26.620
So, we'll let him vote first.

01:04:26.720 --> 01:04:28.120
To be clear, that's Sam from the pod.

01:04:28.220 --> 01:04:29.620
I don't know if everyone knows Sam's last name.

01:04:30.140 --> 01:04:31.220
That's Sam from the podcast.

01:04:31.440 --> 01:04:32.880
Not Sam Smith. Wait, you made a vote.

01:04:33.040 --> 01:04:34.780
His last name. That is really funny, though, too.

01:04:34.900 --> 01:04:39.900
Sam Smith. Like, you know, not the music celebrity.

01:04:40.320 --> 01:04:41.860
Sam Smith Doe. Yeah.

01:04:42.220 --> 01:04:43.380
Oh, I see what you're saying.

01:04:43.540 --> 01:04:44.820
Yeah, yeah. Sam Smith is a...

01:04:44.820 --> 01:04:45.860
I forgot the music man.

01:04:46.040 --> 01:04:46.960
Yes. Very good.

01:04:47.400 --> 01:04:50.700
Anyway, the correct Sam Smith wrote in to say,

01:04:59.400 --> 01:05:13.000
Sam Smith is a...

01:05:13.000 --> 01:05:15.060
Sam should write in to the show more often.

01:05:15.320 --> 01:05:18.920
It's very succinct and well thought out.

01:05:20.940 --> 01:05:22.760
Sean, what do you got?

01:05:24.200 --> 01:05:29.040
So, I had way more fun with this game than I thought I would.

01:05:29.220 --> 01:05:31.120
I had never heard of Gunsmoke.

01:05:31.120 --> 01:05:41.960
While I do more recently like the Western setting, even just scanning by and seeing this on the list of games coming up didn't trigger anything in me.

01:05:43.200 --> 01:05:47.720
And I was really put off by the fact that this was going to be similar to Commando.

01:05:47.800 --> 01:05:51.420
But no, this game was like a hell of a lot of fun to play.

01:05:52.660 --> 01:06:00.680
I thought that while it wasn't always perfect, the changes to the controls were very unique and I guess creative in a way.

01:06:01.700 --> 01:06:04.360
I got into that flow very often.

01:06:04.360 --> 01:06:15.780
But I don't know if it's enough for the essential games list simply because there are so many other choices for good shmups.

01:06:18.700 --> 01:06:21.500
I will agree with Sam that this is definitely a play.

01:06:21.580 --> 01:06:26.180
I think it's in the Hall of Very Good Games instead of the Hall of Fame.

01:06:28.460 --> 01:06:32.620
But I'll continue to play this, but I don't know how long I will.

01:06:33.280 --> 01:06:35.520
Okay. Very well said.

01:06:36.020 --> 01:06:37.540
Joe? So, I'll be honest.

01:06:37.700 --> 01:06:40.400
I actually really struggled with this decision for a while.

01:06:40.400 --> 01:06:42.680
I went back and forth quite a bit on this.

01:06:43.840 --> 01:06:45.320
I loved this game.

01:06:45.320 --> 01:06:50.820
I mean, it's like I said, for a shmup to get that response out of me takes a lot.

01:06:50.940 --> 01:06:53.240
Or maybe just shmups haven't been that great so far.

01:06:53.380 --> 01:06:54.580
Maybe I need to play more of them.

01:06:54.940 --> 01:07:03.360
But just having a unique setting, I think part of that kind of already gives me this like feeling of like, oh, this is different and it's fun and it's new.

01:07:04.180 --> 01:07:13.380
I think that there's just – if I look at the essential games list now and the games we have on there and I look at this game, I think it's like –

01:07:13.380 --> 01:07:19.940
it just doesn't quite meet that level of greatness that these other games have had.

01:07:20.240 --> 01:07:27.540
And if I were alone making the essential games list, this game would be an honorable mention.

01:07:27.740 --> 01:07:29.520
Like that's how close it is to me.

01:07:29.660 --> 01:07:32.500
I think this would be like so close but like just doesn't make the mark.

01:07:35.300 --> 01:07:42.500
So I will – I did land on – I'm going to say it's a golden play it, which I just coined.

01:07:42.740 --> 01:07:48.620
I love that. Stick around for Joe's own podcast where he gives out golden play it.

01:07:50.080 --> 01:07:52.380
And I recommend you check this game out.

01:07:52.540 --> 01:07:58.760
I just don't know if it's quite on the level where I can say this is an essential game in 2019.

01:07:59.540 --> 01:08:05.900
Not to ramble, but I do think if you're playing exclusively NES games from all the games we've played so far, play this one.

01:08:06.100 --> 01:08:08.020
This would be essential on the NES.

01:08:08.740 --> 01:08:15.460
Okay. And to be clear, when Sean voted it out, that was the moment that it couldn't even be on the essential games list.

01:08:15.540 --> 01:08:18.060
So because of our new rules where it has to be majority.

01:08:19.120 --> 01:08:26.660
I will keep mine brief, but I have to say that for me, I like to rank every NES game I play.

01:08:27.440 --> 01:08:32.680
And currently looking at this, it has dethroned Stinger on my list.

01:08:32.820 --> 01:08:39.020
Oh, my. And I consider Stinger essential, so this must be essential to me if I'm playing by my own rules.

01:08:39.220 --> 01:08:40.300
Or we take Stinger off.

01:08:40.500 --> 01:08:44.140
Right. And so – no, it's a good conversation, and I think it's worth having.

01:08:44.440 --> 01:08:46.780
Not something we can do on this particular episode.

01:08:46.780 --> 01:08:53.280
But I think that for me, Gunsmoke is essential at this moment looking at this list.

01:08:53.500 --> 01:08:59.240
I feel very – I feel like this is one of the most fun games that I've had on the NES so far.

01:08:59.480 --> 01:09:10.380
And I think that it's been an incredible time to be an NES gamer right now with Mega Man, Contra, and Gunsmoke all coming up.

01:09:10.380 --> 01:09:19.440
But I will say that obviously Gunsmoke is not in the historical society like how Mega Man and Contra are.

01:09:19.580 --> 01:09:20.980
It's definitely a diamond in the rough, though.

01:09:21.140 --> 01:09:27.280
Right. But it's – yeah, I think as of right now, this has like a kind of like hidden gem or cult status for me.

01:09:27.280 --> 01:09:28.900
That's what I meant. Yeah, for me.

01:09:28.900 --> 01:09:31.520
You know, like I'm feeling that right now.

01:09:31.640 --> 01:09:37.800
So, you know, it's kind of cheap because there's no consequences to me voting it essential because it doesn't wind up on our list.

01:09:37.800 --> 01:09:41.640
But I have to admit that it's doing more for me than Stinger is at the moment.

01:09:41.680 --> 01:09:43.460
And so I'm definitely going to say it's essential.

01:09:44.220 --> 01:09:45.240
All right. Well.

01:09:45.980 --> 01:09:48.660
All right. That is Nostalgia.

01:09:48.800 --> 01:09:52.560
I hope everybody does play Gunsmoke after hearing all of this.

01:09:52.740 --> 01:09:56.140
I don't think it's that expensive of a game when I looked it up.

01:09:56.960 --> 01:10:02.080
But, you know, no one's under pressure to buy games because we talk about them on the podcast.

01:10:02.420 --> 01:10:03.340
But you should.

